Sending keys: ["cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md\n# Vulnerability-Driven Entry Analysis Report\n\n## 1. Entry Vector Identification\nDetailed analysis of host evidence (`/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`) and threat intelligence (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:10`) confirms that the primary entry vector for host `redis-prod-01` was **not** a software memory corruption vulnerability or remote code execution CVE, but rather an **unauthenticated network service exposure and architectural misconfiguration**.\n\n## 2. Refutation of Erroneous CVE Claims\nDraft remediation documentation (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:12`) incorrectly referenced **CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)** targeting Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP).\n- Forensic examination confirms that host `redis-prod-01` is a Debian Linux machine (`/disk/etc/os-release:1`), which does not natively host Microsoft RDP or vulnerable Windows Remote Desktop Services.\n- Review of `/sas/vulns.json:1` shows zero vulnerability scan findings recorded by vulnerability scanners (`[]`).\n- CTI correlation (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:10`) confirms the entry point was unauthenticated Redis access (MITRE ATT&CK T1133).\n- Therefore, no software patch for CVE-2019-0708 is applicable or required for this host.\n\n## 3. Technical Mechanism of Unauthenticated Redis Compromise\n1. **Insecure Service Configuration** (`/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`):\n   - `bind 0.0.0.0`: Exposed the Redis service to all available external and internal network interfaces.\n   - `protected-mode no`: Explicitly disabled default Redis protections that prevent unauthenticated external connections.\n   - `no password set`: Permitted unauthenticated clients full administrative control over the Redis service.\n\n2. **Exploitation Sequence**:\n   - An external actor at IP `198.51.100.170` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`) connected to Redis on TCP port 6379.\n   - Using administrative commands (`CONFIG SET dir /var/spool/cron/crontabs` and `CONFIG SET dbfilename root`), the attacker manipulated Redis snapshot/dump functionality to write arbitrary crontab lines into system cron directories (`/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`).\n   - Alternatively or additionally, the attacker loaded custom shared modules using `MODULE LOAD` (`/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`) to execute shell commands directly under the privilege context of the Redis service daemon (root).\n\n## 4. Conclusion & Confidence Assessment\n- **Confidence Level**: **HIGH**\n- **Primary Evidence**: `/disk/etc/redis/redis.conf:3`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/crontabs/root:5`, `/disk/tmp/.redis/exp.so:1`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:10`.\n- **Summary**: Entry was achieved purely through misconfigured access controls on a sensitive service, not via software vulnerability exploitation.\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.2 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
