Sending keys: ["cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline & Configuration Weakness Report\n\n## System Baseline Assessment\nAn evaluation of baseline configurations and security controls on `rdp-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`) running Windows Server 2022 (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/SOFTWARE:3`) revealed several critical configuration defects that facilitated initial access, enabled brute-force exploitation, and reduced forensic visibility.\n\n---\n\n## Identified Baseline Defects\n\n### 1. Disabled Network Level Authentication (NLA) for Remote Desktop Protocol\n- **Finding ID**: `CHK-06` / `BAS-01` (`/sas/baseline.json:3`, `/sas/alerts.json:27`).\n- **Configuration Registry Path**: `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4` \n- **Current State**: `UserAuthentication = 0` (NLA Disabled, connections allowed).\n- **Security Impact**:\n  - Disabling NLA permits unauthenticated clients to initiate full TLS/RDP handshakes and render the Windows logon interface before authenticating.\n  - This exposes the host to remote credential brute-forcing (ATT&CK T1110) without triggering pre-authentication kerberos/NLA checks or network-level blocking (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`).\n- **Recommended Baseline Standard**:\n  - Enforce `UserAuthentication = 1` (`fPromptForPassword = 1`, `UserAuthentication = 1`) via Group Policy (`HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\\WinStations\\RDP-Tcp`).\n\n### 2. Direct Exposure of RDP Service to Untrusted Public Networks\n- **Finding ID**: Network exposure baseline failure (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/sas/alerts.json:27`).\n- **Configuration Context**: RDP port (3389) accessible directly from public internet IP addresses including attacker source `198.51.100.92` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`).\n- **Security Impact**: Directly exposing RDP port 3389 without access control lists (ACLs), VPN requirements, or IP whitelisting allows continuous brute-force attempts from external threat actors.\n- **Recommended Baseline Standard**: Restrict RDP access to management VPN ranges or administrative bastion hosts via Host Firewall and Network Security Groups.\n\n### 3. Inadequate Local Event Log Tamper Resistance & Central Log Forwarding\n- **Finding ID**: Anti-forensic log clearance vulnerability (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2`).\n- **Configuration Context**: Security event logs were stored solely locally on disk without real-time forwarding to a remote SIEM or syslog server (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2`).\n- **Security Impact**:\n  - The attacker was able to clear the Security Event Log (`EventID 1102`) (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2`).\n  - Because log forwarding was not configured, clearing local event logs destroyed the audit trail of process execution, user privileges, and command-line activity.\n- **Recommended Baseline Standard**: Enable Windows Event Forwarding (WEF) or agent-based log streaming to an isolated remote SIEM server with append-only access controls.\n\n---\n\n## Baseline Compliance Summary Table\n\n| Control ID | Configuration Check | Standard Baseline | Current Non-Compliant State | Citation |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| BAS-01 (CHK-06) | RDP Network Level Authentication | NLA Required (`UserAuthentication=1`) | NLA Disabled (`UserAuthentication=0`) | `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4` |\n| BAS-02 | RDP Perimeter Security | Restricted to VPN / Bastion | Open to Public Internet (`198.51.100.92`) | `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6` |\n| BAS-03 | Audit Log Retention | Remote Forwarding (SIEM) | Local-only, Cleared (EventID 1102) | `/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx:2` |\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
