Sending keys: ["cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Baseline & Security Configuration Assessment\n\n## 1. Executive Summary\nThe forensic evaluation of `smb-prod-01` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`) revealed multiple baseline security configuration deficiencies and missing hardening controls. These weaknesses enabled initial exploitation via legacy SMBv1, credential dumping, stealth persistence, and unmonitored event log clearing (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`, `/sas/baseline.json:2`).\n\n## 2. Identified Baseline & Configuration Deficiencies\n\n### 1. Enablement of Legacy SMBv1 Protocol (BAS-01)\n- **Check ID**: CHK-07 / BAS-01 (`/sas/baseline.json:2`, `/sas/alerts.json:26`)\n- **Finding**: SMBv1 protocol driver `srv.sys` is active and missing security patches (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`, `/sas/baseline.json:2`).\n- **Risk**: Legacy SMBv1 lacks modern security controls and is vulnerable to remote unauthenticated buffer overflows (CVE-2017-0144 / MS17-010).\n- **Baseline Fix**: Disable SMBv1 protocol globally via PowerShell/Group Policy (`Disable-WindowsOptionalFeature -Online -FeatureName SMB1Protocol`) and ensure MS17-010 security update is applied (`/disk/Windows/System32/drivers/srv.sys`).\n\n### 2. Lack of WMI Event Subscription Auditing & Integrity Guardrails\n- **Finding**: Threat actor registered a malicious `__EventFilter` and `CommandLineEventConsumer` in the WMI repository (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`).\n- **Risk**: Unmonitored WMI event subscriptions provide persistent execution under SYSTEM privileges without creating traditional startup entries or service alerts.\n- **Baseline Fix**: Enable WMI activity auditing (Event ID 5861 in WMI-Activity log), restrict WMI namespace permissions, and implement baseline monitoring for repository modifications (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/Repository/OBJECTS.DATA`).\n\n### 3. Insecure Application Search Path / Sideloading Weakness\n- **Finding**: Attacker placed a hijacked DLL `wbemcomn.dll` in `/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`.\n- **Risk**: Search-order hijacking allows malicious code execution whenever WMI or system services attempt to load missing or higher-priority DLLs.\n- **Baseline Fix**: Restrict directory write permissions, enable Safe DLL Search Mode, and enforce AppLocker/WDAC code signing policies (`/disk/Windows/System32/wbem/wbemcomn.dll`).\n\n### 4. Insufficient Event Log Auditing & Protection Controls\n- **Finding**: Attacker cleared System event log (`EventID 1102`) silently without triggering security alerts (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`).\n- **Risk**: Log clearing destroys forensic artifacts and hides ongoing post-exploitation activities.\n- **Baseline Fix**: Configure central SIEM event forwarding, restrict Event Log clearing permissions, and alert on Event ID 1102 / Event ID 104 (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/System.evtx`).\n\n### 5. LSASS Memory Protection Deficiency\n- **Finding**: Attacker dumped LSASS process memory into `/disk/Windows/Temp/lsass.dmp` (`/sas/alerts.json:10`).\n- **Risk**: Unprotected LSASS allows plaintext credential and ticket extraction.\n- **Baseline Fix**: Enable LSA Protection (RunAsPPL) in registry and enable Credential Guard (`/disk/Windows/Temp/lsass.dmp`).\nEOF\n"] min_timeout_sec: 0.5 max_timeout_sec: 180.0
